From 0a0ec481f7122f76c99d7fcb3a1f245c485be593 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 20:57:24 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Why is the Arctic surface layer too salty? Closure option,
 two 30-day tests, and profiles

The Sep-Oct 2005 run keeps the right freshwater content in the Arctic but its
surface is 0.2-0.7 too salty: fresh water mixed down. Two tests, 30 days each
(September 2005, 15 km), compared with the 61-day run's September:

- panarctic.jl: [ocean] closure = "CATKE" (default, unchanged), "RiBased" or
  "TKEDissipation"; config_sep2005_30d_ribased.toml uses RiBased.
- config_sep2005_30d_nz100.toml: 100 levels (top cell ~4 m instead of ~8 m).

analysis/salinity_drift.jl also draws region-mean model - GLORYS profiles of
salinity and temperature over the top 300 m (salinity_drift_profiles.png): a
salty surface over a fresher layer below is the sign of too much mixing.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Abhhvm7xByUXxouq5u8r5W
